"{#} to {#} minutes per view",
"An ionic crystal holds together because each ion sits in the net field of all the others: the Coulomb energy per ion pair is −α ke²/r₀, and the Madelung constant α ({#} for NaCl) is a property of the geometry alone. The simple model U = α ke²/r₀ ({#} − {#}/n) gets the measured lattice energies to within about {#} %.",
"Bands are what atomic levels become when N atoms share their electrons: each level splits into exactly N levels, spread over a width set by how much neighboring orbitals overlap. Counting electrons into those levels decides metal or insulator.",
"In a semiconductor the carrier density is set by Boltzmann factors: n_i ∝ T^({#}/{#}) e^(−E_g/2kT) for a pure crystal, so the band gap can be read from a graph; a part in fifty million of phosphorus fixes n at N_D over a wide range of temperature.",
"A pn junction passes current one way because forward bias lowers the barrier by eV and the current grows by e^(eV/kT); an LED's photon energy is about the band gap.",
"Students should have met Coulomb potential energy, the Boltzmann factor and the idea of energy levels in atoms. The demo has four views. <strong>Crystal</strong>: a salt crystal in 3D around a reference ion, with the Madelung sum added up by shells or by Evjen's neutral cubes, and the {#} alkali halides of the book's Table {#}. <strong>Bands</strong>: a chain of N model atoms (tight binding, s and p levels) whose levels are drawn against the spacing and filled with electrons. <strong>Doping</strong>: silicon, germanium or gallium arsenide in a cryostat from {#} K to {#} K, with donors and acceptors in silicon, a band diagram and the carrier densities. <strong>Junction</strong>: an ideal silicon pn junction with a battery and an ammeter, its depletion region and band diagram, and an LED mode.",
"<strong>Rock salt.</strong> Show the reference ion and its {#} nearest neighbors. Add shells one at a time (Q1): the total swings wildly. Switch to neutral cubes: {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}. Then compare {#} eV per ion pair ({#} kJ/mol) with the table's {#} kJ/mol (Q2).",
"<strong>Cesium chloride.</strong> Eight nearest neighbors; α = {#}. Ask why most alkali halides still take the NaCl structure (Q3).",
"<strong>Levels become bands.</strong> Six atoms at {#} nm: six nearly equal levels. Squeeze them to {#} nm and watch each level fan out into six (Q4).",
