"<strong>Periodic potential.</strong> In <strong>Bands</strong>, switch the Model to <strong>Periodic potential (Kronig–Penney)</strong>: one electron in a row of wells {#} nm wide between {#} eV barriers {#} nm thick. Band {#} runs from {#} to {#} eV and the first gap is {#} eV wide; step k from {#} to π/a and watch the state move along its band while E against k bends away from the free electron parabola. <strong>Deep wells, narrow bands</strong> ({#} eV, {#} nm barriers) shrinks band {#} to {#} to {#} eV, toward the level of a single well; lower V₀ to {#} and the gaps close.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> The book writes the Madelung constant of NaCl as {#} − {#}/√{#} + {#}/√{#} − {#}/{#} + {#}/√{#} − ... Add the first three and the first five terms. Why does adding shells one at a time not settle on {#}, and what does the neutral cube method do differently?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> For NaCl, r₀ = {#} nm and n = {#}. Find the lattice energy in eV per ion pair and in kJ/mol from U = α ke²/r₀ ({#} − {#}/n), and compare with the measured {#} kJ/mol. What does the model leave out?",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> The CsCl structure has a larger Madelung constant ({#}) than the NaCl structure ({#}). Why, then, do NaCl, KCl and most other alkali halides take the NaCl structure?",
